  "raw": {
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/dcurecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=003496": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Business Analytics - AI and Agentic Systems",
      "description": "Closing Date: 19/10/2026 Assistant Professor in Business Analytics - AI and Agentic Systems Applications are invited from suitably qualified candidates for the following position #003496 Assistant Professor in Business Analytics - AI and Agentic Systems, 3-Year-Fixed Term Contract Role Profile The successful individual will be a member of the Enterprise and Innovation Group in DCU Business School. They will conduct high quality research and teaching in Business Analytics, with a strong emphasis on analytics and AI for optimising business decision making, productivity, and business value. The role requires experience in the design, operationalisation, and teaching of AI and agentic AI workflows, including the translation of emerging AI capabilities into robust, responsible, and measurable business processes. The successful individual will also contribute expertise in deep learning for natural language processing (NLP), including practical applications such as text analytics, semantic search, information extraction, conversational interfaces, retrieval-augmented generation, and decision-support systems. They will also participate in the School’s external engagement activities. Further, the successful individual is expected to contribute to programme management, research supervision and the service activities of the School and University. The successful candidate will contribute to the School’s growing activities in analytics, AI, and digital transformation. Please see the attached job description which includes details on the responsibilities associated with the role and the qualifications and experience required. Salary Scale: Assistant Professor - €65,229 - €104,536 Appointment will be commensurate with qualifications and experience and in line with current Government pay policy. Closing Date: Monday, 19th October 2026 For more information on the School, please visit DCU Business School Informal Enquiries in relation to this role should be directed to: Professor Regina Connolly, Head of the Enterprise and Innovation Group, Dublin City University Business School, Dublin City University. Email: Regina.Connolly@dcu.ie Please do not send applications to this email address, instead apply as described below. Application Procedure: Please submit your application through the online system. In order to be considered for the role to which you are applying for, you must upload: 1) Curriculum Vitae 2) Cover Letter 3) Completed application form (blank forms can be downloaded from the bottom of the Vacancy). Please note, if all items are not uploaded, the application will be deemed incomplete, and will not be processed. Dublin City University is an equal opportunities employer. In line with the Employment Equality Acts 1998 – 2015, the University is committed to equality of treatment for all those who engage with its recruitment, selection and appointment processes. We will provide support and accommodations to ensure all candidates, including those with disabilities, can fully participate in the process. The University’s Athena Swan Silver Award signifies the University’s commitment to promoting gender equality and addressing any gender pay gaps. Information on a range of university policies aimed at creating a supportive and flexible work environment are available in the DCU Policy Starter Packs #LI-Hybrid Contact Email : talent@dcu.ie Contact No : +00353 1 700 5436"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/dcurecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=003503": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Psychology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Assistant Professor in Psychology Applications are now invited for the position of #003503 Assistant Professor in Psychology, Permanent Contract Role Profile We are seeking to recruit an Assistant Professor in Psychology on a permanent contract who will report to the Head of School of Psychology. The successful candidate will have a strong academic track record in an area of psychology that complements the existing teaching and research strengths of the School. The successful candidate will contribute high-quality teaching in an area such as social psychology. The successful candidate will also lead a high-quality research programme and actively participate in the School’s external engagement and enterprise activities. Further, the successful candidate will contribute to programme administration, placement supervision, research supervision and the external engagement activities of the School and University. This post provides an exciting opportunity for the successful candidate to play a key role in the further development of the School’s success and national and international reputation. Please see the attached job description which outlines the responsibilities associated with the role and the qualifications and experience required. Salary Scale: Assistant Professor Salary Scale - € 65,229 - € 104,536 Appointment will be commensurate with qualifications and experience and in line with current Government pay policy. Closing date : Monday, 9th November 2026 For more information on DCU and our benefits, please visit Why work at DCU? Informal Enquiries in relation to this role should be directed to: Professor Sinéad Smyth, Head of School, School of Psychology, Faculty of Science and Health, Dublin City University. Phone + 353 (0)1 7007422 Email: sinead.smyth@dcu.ie Please do not send applications to this email address, instead apply as described below. Application Procedure: Please submit your application through the online system at www.dcu.ie/people/jobs, in order to be considered for the role to which you are applying for, you must upload: 1) Curriculum Vitae 2) Cover Letter 3) Completed application form (blank forms can be downloaded from the bottom of the Vacancy). Please note, if all items are not uploaded, the application will be deemed incomplete, and will not be processed. Dublin City University is an equal opportunities employer. In line with the Employment Equality Acts 1998 – 2015, the University is committed to equality of treatment for all those who engage with its recruitment, selection and appointment processes. The University’s Athena Swan Silver Award signifies the University’s commitment to promoting gender equality and addressing any gender pay gaps. Information on a range of university policies aimed at creating a supportive and flexible work environment are available in the DCU Policy Starter Packs #LI-Hybrid Contact Email : talent@dcu.ie Contact No : +00353 1 700 5149"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/dcurecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=003499": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Health/Social Care",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Postdoctoral Researcher in Health/Social Care Applications are now invited for the position of #003499 Postdoctoral Researcher in Health/Social Care, 45 Month Fixed Term Contract (Full-time) Role Profile Applications are invited for the post-doctoral position (point 2) to work on a Health Research Board-Health Service Executive funded study on peer-to-peer abuse and aggression in residential care for adults. The successful candidate will work closely with an interdisciplinary team to develop training and guidance on the prevention and management of peer-to-peer abuse in nursing homes, residential care facilities for people with intellectual disability and mental health residential care. Adult safeguarding is concerned with ensuring individuals' autonomy and self-determination are supported and protecting adults at risk of abuse and neglect. Peer-to-peer abuse and aggression (PPAA) is defined as “offensive, aggressive, and intrusive verbal, physical, sexual, and material interactions between service users that in a community setting would likely be unwelcome and potentially cause physical or psychological distress or harm to the recipient/victim”. Despite representing the most common referral concern to the Health Service Executive, there has been no Irish research, while global studies are scant, with most focusing on nursing homes. This study will explore how PPAA is experienced in Irish adult residential care settings and will develop prevention and early intervention guidance for PPAA in nursing homes, and residential care facilities for people with mental health difficulties and people with intellectual disabilities. The study is a sequential mixed-method design enabling the generation of praxis-based deliverables to improve quality and patient safety related to PPAA. It is supported by a participatory action research framework that centres on working with people’s experiences towards co-designed, emancipatory change. Data collection has five stages: 1) Rapid realist review, retrospective chart review, 2) Interviews, 3) Participatory Action Research groups, 4) Expert consensus and World Cafes, and 5) Project impact and collaboration evaluation. Please see the attached job description which outlines the responsibilities associated with the role and the qualifications and experience required. Salary Scale: IUA Postdoctoral Researcher Salary Scale – (Point 1 to 2) €47,273 - €47,879 Appointment will be commensurate with qualifications and experience and in line with current Government pay policy. Closing date: Sunday, 18th October 2026 For more information on DCU and our benefits, please visit Why work at DCU? Informal Enquiries in relation to this role should be directed to: Professor Amanda Phelan, School of Nursing, Psychotherapy & Community Health, Faculty of Science and Health, Dublin City University. Phone + 353 (0)1 7008025, Email: Amanda.phelan@dcu.ie Please do not send applications to this email address, instead apply as described below. Application Procedure: Please submit your application through the online system at www.dcu.ie/people/jobs, in order to be considered for the role to which you are applying for, you must upload: 1) Curriculum Vitae 2) Cover Letter 3) Completed application form (blank forms can be downloaded from the bottom of the Vacancy). Please note, if all items are not uploaded, the application will be deemed incomplete, and will not be processed. Dublin City University is an equal opportunities employer. In line with the Employment Equality Acts 1998 – 2015, the University is committed to equality of treatment for all those who engage with its recruitment, selection and appointment processes. The University’s Athena Swan Silver Award signifies the University’s commitment to promoting gender equality and addressing any gender pay gaps. Information on a range of university policies aimed at creating a supportive and flexible work environment are available in the DCU Policy Starter Packs #LI-Hybrid Contact Email : talent@dcu.ie Contact No : +00353 1 700 5149"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/dcurecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=003502": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Women’s Health (Endometriosis)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 25/10/2026 Postdoctoral Researcher in Women’s Health (Endometriosis) Applications are now invited for the position of #003502 Postdoctoral Researcher in Women’s Health (Endometriosis), 2 Year Fixed Term Contract Role Profile Applications are invited from enthusiastic and suitably qualified candidates for this 24-month fixed term contract as a Postdoctoral Researcher at the School of Health and Human Performance. Endometriosis affects approximately one in ten women in Ireland, yet care continues to be defined by diagnostic delays and fragmented healthcare pathways. This research project aims to support the operationalisation of the National Framework for the Management of Endometriosis in Ireland (2025). The successful candidate will work across a four-stage research design comprising a Delphi consensus study, the co-design of a digital decision-support tool for general practitioners, an evaluation of this tool's impact on treatment pathways and patient satisfaction, and a pilot of the shared medical appointments model. The post is based on the Glasnevin Campus of Dublin City University, and the appointee will report to Professor Siobhán O’Connor, the Principal Investigator. Please see the attached job description which outlines the responsibilities associated with the role and the qualifications and experience required. Salary Scale: IUA Postdoctoral Researcher Salary Scale Point 1 - €47,273 Appointment will be commensurate with qualifications and experience and in line with current Government pay policy. Closing date: Sunday, 25th of October 2026. For more information on DCU and our benefits, please visit Why work at DCU? Informal Enquiries in relation to this role should be directed to: Professor Siobhán O’Connor, School of Health and Human Performance, Faculty of Science and Health, Dublin City University. Phone + 353 (0)1 7008579, Email: siobhan.oconnor@dcu.ie Please do not send applications to this email address, instead apply as described below. Application Procedure: Please submit your application through the online system at www.dcu.ie/people/jobs, in order to be considered for the role to which you are applying for, you must upload: 1) Curriculum Vitae 2) Cover Letter 3) Completed application form (blank forms can be downloaded from the bottom of the Vacancy). Please note, if all items are not uploaded, the application will be deemed incomplete, and will not be processed. Dublin City University is an equal opportunities employer. In line with the Employment Equality Acts 1998 – 2015, the University is committed to equality of treatment for all those who engage with its recruitment, selection and appointment processes. The University’s Athena Swan Silver Award signifies the University’s commitment to promoting gender equality and addressing any gender pay gaps. Information on a range of university policies aimed at creating a supportive and flexible work environment are available in the DCU Policy Starter Packs #LI-Hybrid Contact Email : talent@dcu.ie Contact No : +00353 1 700 5149"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/dcurecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=003500": {
      "title": "Research Assistant in Health/Social Care",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Research Assistant in Health/Social Care Applications are now invited for the position of #003500 Research Assistant in Health/Social Care, 48 months (Part-time 0.2 FTE,) 1 day per week, flexible Role Profile Applications are invited for the part-time research assistant position (level 1, point 2 at 0.2 WTE) to work on a Health Research Board-Health Service Executive funded study on peer-to-peer abuse and aggression in residential care for adults. The successful candidate will work closely with an interdisciplinary team to develop training and guidance on the prevention and management of peer-to-peer abuse in nursing homes, residential care facilities for people with intellectual disability and mental health residential care. Adult safeguarding is concerned with ensuring individuals' autonomy and self-determination are supported and protecting adults at risk of abuse and neglect. Peer-to-peer abuse and aggression (PPAA) is defined as “offensive, aggressive, and intrusive verbal, physical, sexual, and material interactions between service users that in a community setting would likely be unwelcome and potentially cause physical or psychological distress or harm to the recipient/victim”. Despite representing the most common referral concern to the Health Service Executive, there has been no Irish research, while global studies are scant, with most focusing on nursing homes. This study will explore how PPAA is experienced in Irish adult residential care settings and will develop prevention and early intervention guidance for PPAA in nursing homes, and residential care facilities for people with mental health difficulties and people with intellectual disabilities. The study is a sequential mixed-method design enabling the generation of praxis-based deliverables to improve quality and patient safety related to PPAA. It is supported by a participatory action research framework that centres on working with people’s experiences towards co-designed, emancipatory change. Data collection has five stages: 1) Rapid realist review, retrospective chart review, 2) Interviews, 3) Participatory Action Research groups, 4) Expert consensus and World Cafes, and 5) Project impact and collaboration evaluation. Please see the attached job description which outlines the responsibilities associated with the role and the qualifications and experience required. Salary Scale: IUA Research Assistant Salary Scale – (point 1 to 2) €34,129 - €34,966 (Pro-rata for 0.2 FTE) Appointment will be commensurate with qualifications and experience and in line with current Government pay policy. Closing date: Sunday, 18th October 2026 For more information on DCU and our benefits, please visit Why work at DCU? Informal Enquiries in relation to this role should be directed to: Professor Amanda Phelan, School of Nursing, Psychotherapy & Community Health, Faculty of Science and Health, Dublin City University. Phone + 353 (0)1 7008025, Email: Amanda.phelan@dcu.ie Please do not send applications to this email address, instead apply as described below. Application Procedure: Please submit your application through the online system at www.dcu.ie/people/jobs, in order to be considered for the role to which you are applying for, you must upload: 1) Curriculum Vitae 2) Cover Letter 3) Completed application form (blank forms can be downloaded from the bottom of the Vacancy). Please note, if all items are not uploaded, the application will be deemed incomplete, and will not be processed. Dublin City University is an equal opportunities employer. In line with the Employment Equality Acts 1998 – 2015, the University is committed to equality of treatment for all those who engage with its recruitment, selection and appointment processes. The University’s Athena Swan Silver Award signifies the University’s commitment to promoting gender equality and addressing any gender pay gaps. Information on a range of university policies aimed at creating a supportive and flexible work environment are available in the DCU Policy Starter Packs Contact Email : talent@dcu.ie Contact No : +00353 1 700 5149"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/dcurecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=003501": {
      "title": "Senior Data Analyst",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/10/2026 Senior Data Analyst Applications are invited from suitably qualified candidates for the following position #003501 Senior Data Analyst, 3 Year Fixed Term Contract Role Profile Reporting to the Head of Curriculum Management Service (CMS), the Senior Data Analyst is responsible for the design, development, and continuous improvement of operational reporting services supporting the student lifecycle across Admissions, Registration, Curriculum Management, Examinations, Progression, and Graduation. The role provides advanced technical expertise within the Operational Reporting function, ensuring the delivery of accurate, reliable, secure, and timely reporting solutions through ARGOS, SQL, Power BI, and related reporting platforms. The post holder will input and support reporting development standards, quality assurance processes, data governance practices, and capability development within the team while supporting the delivery of strategic reporting improvements and reporting modernisation initiatives. Working within a small team, the Senior Data Analyst will translate business requirements into robust reporting solutions, provide technical oversight across all reporting activities, and ensure operational continuity through knowledge sharing and documentation. The Head of Curriculum Management Service reports to the Senior Director of Academic Governance, Operations and Systems, OVPAA. Please see the attached job description which includes details on the responsibilities associated with the role and the qualifications and experience required. Salary Scale: Professional 5A: €60,580 - €81,922 Appointment will be commensurate with qualifications and experience and in line with current Government pay policy. Closing Date: Friday, 16th October 2026 Additional Information Please note this role does not meet criteria for a Critical Skills permit as stipulated by the Department of Enterprise, Trade & Employment. For more information on the Office of the Vice-President of Academic Affairs (Registrar) - https://www.dcu.ie/ovpaa Informal Enquiries in relation to this role should be directed to: Ms Bernadette Dowling, Curriculum Management Services, Dublin City University. Phone + 353 (0)1 700 7724 Email: bernadette.dowling@dcu.ie Please do not send applications to this email address, instead apply as described below. Application Procedure: Please submit your application through the online system. In order to be considered for the role to which you are applying for, you must upload: 1) Curriculum Vitae 2) Cover Letter 3) Completed application form (blank forms can be downloaded from the bottom of the Vacancy). Please note, if all items are not uploaded, the application will be deemed incomplete, and will not be processed. Dublin City University is an equal opportunities employer. In line with the Employment Equality Acts 1998 – 2015, the University is committed to equality of treatment for all those who engage with its recruitment, selection and appointment processes. We will provide support and accommodations to ensure all candidates, including those with disabilities, can fully participate in the process. The University’s Athena Swan Silver Award signifies the University’s commitment to promoting gender equality and addressing any gender pay gaps. Information on a range of university policies aimed at creating a supportive and flexible work environment are available in the DCU Policy Starter Packs #LI-Hybrid Contact Email : talent@dcu.ie Contact No : +00353 1 700 5436"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/dcurecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=003498": {
      "title": "Technical Officer/ Senior Technical Officer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Technical Officer/ Senior Technical Officer Applications are now invited for the position of #003498 Technical Officer/ Senior Technical Officer, 3 Year Fixed Term Contract Role Profile The Research Infrastructure Unit is seeking to recruit an experienced and motivated Technical Officer or Senior Technical Officer to oversee and operate our multi-platform mass spectrometry core facility. Reporting to the RIU Chief Technical Officer, the successful candidate will join the current technical support team and will have specific responsibilities for certain research infrastructure as well as more generic technical duties supporting day to day research activities. This person will also be responsible for the continued development and implementation of the equipment quality systems for facilities associated with DCU’s priority research areas. Please see the attached job description which outlines the responsibilities associated with the role and the qualifications and experience required. Salary Scale: Technical Officer / Senior Technical Officer - €42,153- €74,920 Appointment will be commensurate with qualifications and experience and in line with current Government pay policy. Closing date: Sunday, 18th October 2026 For more information on DCU and our benefits, please visit ** Why work at DCU ** Informal Enquiries in relation to this role should be directed to: Mr. Robbie Sinnott, Research Infrastructure Unit, Dublin City University. Phone + 353 (0)1 7006320 Email: robbie.sinnott@dcu.ie Please do not send applications to this email address, instead apply as described below. Application Procedure: Please submit your application through the online system at www.dcu.ie/people/jobs, in order to be considered for the role to which you are applying for, you must upload: 1) Curriculum Vitae 2) Cover Letter 3) Completed application form (blank forms can be downloaded from the bottom of the Vacancy). Please note, if all items are not uploaded, the application will be deemed incomplete, and will not be processed. Dublin City University is an equal opportunities employer. In line with the Employment Equality Acts 1998 – 2015, the University is committed to equality of treatment for all those who engage with its recruitment, selection and appointment processes. The University’s Athena Swan Silver Award signifies the University’s commitment to promoting gender equality and addressing any gender pay gaps. Information on a range of university policies aimed at creating a supportive and flexible work environment are available in the DCU Policy Starter Packs #LI-Hybrid Contact Email : talent@dcu.ie Contact No : +00353 1 700 5149"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/dcurecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=003505": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Education: Placement Coordinator",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Assistant Professor in Education: Placement Coordinator Applications are invited from suitably qualified candidates for the following position #003505 Assistant Professor in Education: Placement Coordinator, Permanent Contract Role Profile The School of Policy and Practice is seeking to recruit an Assistant Professor in Education with particular expertise in a) the role of professional placement in initial teacher education and b) professional studies in education. A significant element of the role will involve the coordination and development of school placement across IoE and DCU post-primary teacher education programmes as part of a team of school placement coordinators. Familiarity with recent and emerging trends in Irish post-primary education will be important. Please see the attached job description which includes details on the responsibilities associated with the role and the qualifications and experience required. Salary Scale: Assistant Professor (Above Bar): €65,229 - €104,536 *Candidates must have a PhD to be placed on the above bar scale Appointment will be commensurate with qualifications and experience and in line with current Government pay policy. Closing date: Friday, 23rd October 2026 For more information on the School of Policy and Practice please visit School of Policy and Practice Informal Enquiries in relation to this role should be directed to: Dr Jane O’Kelly, Head of School of Policy and Practice, DCU Institute of Education, Dublin CityUniversity. Email: jane.okelly@dcu.ie Please do not send applications to this email address, instead apply as described below. Application Procedure: Please submit your application through the online system. In order to be considered for the role to which you are applying for, you must upload: 1) Curriculum Vitae 2) Cover Letter 3) Completed application form (blank forms can be downloaded from the bottom of the Vacancy). Please note, if all items are not uploaded, the application will be deemed incomplete, and will not be processed. Dublin City University is an equal opportunities employer. In line with the Employment Equality Acts 1998 – 2015, the University is committed to equality of treatment for all those who engage with its recruitment, selection and appointment processes. We will provide support and accommodations to ensure all candidates, including those with disabilities, can fully participate in the process. The University’s Athena Swan Silver Award signifies the University’s commitment to promoting gender equality and addressing any gender pay gaps. Information on a range of university policies aimed at creating a supportive and flexible work environment are available in the DCU Policy Starter Packs #LI-Hybrid Contact Email : talent@dcu.ie Contact No : +00353 1 700 5149"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/dcurecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=003508": {
      "title": "DCU Participation Sport Development Officer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/10/2026 DCU Participation Sport Development Officer Applications are invited from suitably qualified candidates for the following position #003508 DCU Participation Sport Development Officer, 3-year Fixed Term Contract Role Profile The Participation Sport Development Officer is responsible for co-ordinating the delivery of the Participation pillar of the DCU Sport & Physical Activity Strategy within DCU’s Sport Development Unit. The postholder will drive increased participation in sport, physical activity, and wellbeing among students by designing, implementing, and evaluating targeted programmes, with a particular emphasis on engaging inactive and less-active populations and under-represented groups. The role will support sports clubs in developing accessible participation pathways and contribute to the sustainable growth and development of the club system. Working collaboratively with internal stakeholders, external partners, and student leaders, the postholder will co-ordinate the delivery of participation growth through evidence-based programme development, strategic partnerships, student activation initiatives, and targeted campaigns that promote the benefits of sport and physical activity, the student experience and belonging. Please see the attached job description which includes details on the responsibilities associated with the role and the qualifications and experience required. Salary Scale: Professional 4: €45,116 - €65,984. Appointment will be commensurate with qualifications and experience and in line with current Government pay policy. Closing Date: Friday, 30th October 2026. Additional Information Please note this role does not meet criteria for a Critical Skills permit as stipulated by the Department of Enterprise, Trade & Employment. For more information on DCU Sport, please visit - https://www.dcu.ie/dcusport Informal Enquiries in relation to this role should be directed to: Yvonne McGowan, Deputy Director of Sport, DCU Sport, Dublin City University. Phone + 353 (0)1 7005811 Email: yvonne.mcgowan@dcu.ie Please do not send applications to this email address, instead apply as described below. Application Procedure: Please submit your application through the online system. In order to be considered for the role to which you are applying for, you must upload: Curriculum Vitae Completed online application form Please note, if all items are not uploaded, the application will be deemed incomplete, and will not be processed. Dublin City University is an equal opportunities employer. In line with the Employment Equality Acts 1998 – 2015, the University is committed to equality of treatment for all those who engage with its recruitment, selection and appointment processes. We will provide support and accommodations to ensure all candidates, including those with disabilities, can fully participate in the process. The University’s Athena Swan Silver Award signifies the University’s commitment to promoting gender equality and addressing any gender pay gaps. Information on a range of university policies aimed at creating a supportive and flexible work environment are available in the DCU Policy Starter Packs #LI-Hybrid talent@dcu.ie +00353 1 700 5436"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/dcurecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=003494": {
      "title": "Recruiter",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/10/2026 Recruiter Applications are invited from suitably qualified candidates for the following position #003494 Recruiter, 1 Year Fixed Term Contract Role Profile Join a purpose-driven Talent Acquisition Team at DCU, where you'll play a key role in shaping the University’s future by recruiting outstanding academic, professional, technical, and research talent. As a Recruiter, you will manage end-to-end recruitment campaigns and act as a trusted partner to hiring managers across a range of disciplines. This is an excellent opportunity to advance your recruitment career by working on complex hiring needs, improving recruitment processes, and enhancing the candidate experience. You will collaborate closely with colleagues in Talent Acquisition, People Partners, and People Services to ensure seamless recruitment and onboarding, all while reinforcing DCU’s people-first and inclusive approach. Please see the attached job description which includes details on the responsibilities associated with the role and the qualifications and experience required. Salary Scale: Professional 3 - €42,893 - €53,367 Appointment will be commensurate with qualifications and experience and in line with current Government pay policy. Closing Date: Friday, 30th October 2026 For more information on DCU and benefits, please visit Why work at DCU? Informal Enquiries in relation to this role should be directed to: Ms Mary Jennings, Senior People Manager, DCU People, Dublin City University. Email: mary.jennings@dcu.ie Please do not send applications to this email address, instead apply as described below. Application Procedure: Please submit your application through the online system. In order to be considered for the role to which you are applying for, you must upload: Curriculum Vitae Completed online application form Please note, if all items are not uploaded, the application will be deemed incomplete, and will not be processed. Dublin City University is an equal opportunities employer. In line with the Employment Equality Acts 1998 – 2015, the University is committed to equality of treatment for all those who engage with its recruitment, selection and appointment processes. We will provide support and accommodations to ensure all candidates, including those with disabilities, can fully participate in the process. The University’s Athena Swan Silver Award signifies the University’s commitment to promoting gender equality and addressing any gender pay gaps. Information on a range of university policies aimed at creating a supportive and flexible work environment are available in the DCU Policy Starter Packs #LI-Hybrid talent@dcu.ie +00353 1 700 5149"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/dcurecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=003493": {
      "title": "Research Assistant in Computational Social Science",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Research Assistant in Computational Social Science Applications are invited from suitably qualified candidates for the following position #003493 Research Assistant in Computational Social Science, 9 Month Fixed Term Contract (Part-time, 0.5 FTE) Role Profile This role is associated with an interdisciplinary Research Ireland funded project and is focused on the design and application of data-driven methods to analyse governance dynamics, generating insights that inform ethical and innovative, evidence-based policy solutions. In addition, the candidate is expected to carry out the day-to-day management activities associated with the execution of the project work-programme, such as qualitative research, data analysis, administrative work and academic writing. Please see the attached job description which includes details on the responsibilities associated with the role and the qualifications and experience required. Salary Scale: Research Assistant: €34,129 - €34,966 (Point 1 -2) Appointment will be commensurate with qualifications and experience and in line with current Government pay policy. Closing Date: Friday, 23rd October 2026 Additional Information Please note this role does not meet criteria for a Critical Skills permit as stipulated by the Department of Enterprise, Trade & Employment. For more information on the Thrive Project, please visit - https://thrive-project.ie/ Informal Enquiries in relation to this role should be directed to: Dr Valesca Lima, Associate Professor of Politics at the School of Law and Government, Dublin City University. Email: project-thrive@dcu.ie Please do not send applications to this email address, instead apply as described below. Application Procedure: Please submit your application through the online system. In order to be considered for the role to which you are applying for, you must upload: 1) Curriculum Vitae 2) Cover Letter 3) Completed application form (blank forms can be downloaded from the bottom of the Vacancy). Please note, if all items are not uploaded, the application will be deemed incomplete, and will not be processed. Dublin City University is an equal opportunities employer. In line with the Employment Equality Acts 1998 – 2015, the University is committed to equality of treatment for all those who engage with its recruitment, selection and appointment processes. We will provide support and accommodations to ensure all candidates, including those with disabilities, can fully participate in the process. The University’s Athena Swan Silver Award signifies the University’s commitment to promoting gender equality and addressing any gender pay gaps. Information on a range of university policies aimed at creating a supportive and flexible work environment are available in the DCU Policy Starter Packs Contact Email : talent@dcu.ie Contact No : + 353 1 700 7991"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/dcurecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=003509": {
      "title": "Research Assistant in Health, Hormones and Home Life",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Research Assistant in Health, Hormones and Home Life Applications are now invited for the position of #003509 Research Assistant in Health, Hormones and Home Life, 14 Month Fixed Term Contract, Part-Time 0.5 FTE Role Profile Applications are invited from enthusiastic and suitably qualified individuals for this 14-month part-time contract as a Research Assistant at the School of Health and Human Performance. This position is available from December 2026. This role offers the unique opportunity to contribute to an innovative, cross-national longitudinal study examining the health and wellbeing of first-time parents across the transition to parenthood. The successful candidate will gain valuable experience in participant engagement, objective sleep assessment, biological sample analysis, and research data management.The post is based on the Glasnevin Campus of Dublin City University, and the appointee will report to the Principal Investigator. Please see the attached job description which outlines the responsibilities associated with the role and the qualifications and experience required. Salary Scale: IUA Research Assistant Salary Scale – €34,129 (Point 1) Appointment will be commensurate with qualifications and experience and in line with current Government pay policy. Closing date: Sunday, 1st November 2026 For more information on the School of Health and Human Performance, please visit: https://www.dcu.ie/shhp Informal Enquiries in relation to this role should be directed to: Dr Anna Donnla O’Hagan, School of Health and Human Performance, Faculty of Science and Health, Dublin City University. Phone: + 353 (0)1 700 6441, Email: annadonnla.ohagan@dcu.ie Please do not send applications to this email address, instead apply as described below. Application Procedure: Please submit your application through the online system at www.dcu.ie/people/jobs, in order to be considered for the role to which you are applying for, you must upload: 1) Curriculum Vitae 2) Cover Letter 3) Completed application form (blank forms can be downloaded from the bottom of the Vacancy). Please note, if all items are not uploaded, the application will be deemed incomplete, and will not be processed. Dublin City University is an equal opportunities employer. In line with the Employment Equality Acts 1998 – 2015, the University is committed to equality of treatment for all those who engage with its recruitment, selection and appointment processes. The University’s Athena Swan Silver Award signifies the University’s commitment to promoting gender equality and addressing any gender pay gaps. Information on a range of university policies aimed at creating a supportive and flexible work environment are available in the DCU Policy Starter Packs #LI-Hybrid Contact Email : talent@dcu.ie Contact No : +00353 1 700 5149"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/dcurecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=003507": {
      "title": "Trainee Analyst Programmer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Trainee Analyst Programmer Applications are invited from suitably qualified candidates for the following position #003507 Trainee Analyst Programmer, u p to 6 Month Fixed Term Contract Role Profile The role of the researcher on the Sport Ireland project will be to perform an audit of the existing digital infrastructure within Sport Ireland and develop recommendations and associated proof of concept pipelines for the digital transformation of Sport Ireland. In addition, the researcher will aid Sport Ireland in assessing sport participation nationally and will develop end-to-end systems to capture and report on sports participation and facilities planning. Please see the attached job description which includes details on the responsibilities associated with the role and the qualifications and experience required. Salary Scale: Trainee Analyst Programmer Salary Scale - €44,898 - €47,223 Appointment will be commensurate with qualifications and experience and in line with current Government pay policy. Closing Date: Tuesday, 20th October 2026 Additional Information Please note this role does not meet criteria for a Critical Skills permit as stipulated by the Department of Enterprise, Trade & Employment. For more information on the School of Computing, please visit : SCOFCOMP Informal Enquiries in relation to this role should be directed to: Dr Michael Scriney, Assistant Professor, School of Computing, Faculty of Engineering and Computing, Dublin City University. Phone: + 353 (0)1 7008728 Email: michael.scriney@dcu.ie Please do not send applications to this email address, instead apply as described below. Application Procedure: Please submit your application through the online system. In order to be considered for the role to which you are applying for, you must upload: 1. Curriculum Vitae 2. Completed online application form Please note, if all items are not uploaded, the application will be deemed incomplete and will not be processed. Dublin City University is an equal opportunities employer. In line with the Employment Equality Acts 1998 – 2015, the University is committed to equality of treatment for all those who engage with its recruitment, selection and appointment processes. We will provide support and accommodations to ensure all candidates, including those with disabilities, can fully participate in the process. The University’s Athena Swan Silver Award signifies the University’s commitment to promoting gender equality and addressing any gender pay gaps. Information on a range of university policies aimed at creating a supportive and flexible work environment are available in the DCU Policy Starter Packs #LI-Hybrid talent@dcu.ie +00353 1 700 5149"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/dcurecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=003512": {
      "title": "Assistant Faculty Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 06/11/2026 Assistant Faculty Manager Applications are invited from suitably qualified candidates for the following position #003512 Assistant Faculty Manager, Permanent Contract Role Profile The Assistant Faculty Manager role is a senior position within the Business School’s professional and administrative team and is central to the effective operation of the school. The post is both managerial and administrative in nature and the post-holder will be expected to make a significant contribution to the attainment of the school’s strategic objectives. The successful candidate will directly manage staff in the school’s professional and administration team and will assist the Faculty Manager with a wide range of operational duties. The specific responsibilities of this role include overseeing academic administration, managing administrative staff (including recruitment, training, and development), driving IT/systems development and implementation, providing leadership on strategically important university or school projects, and providing high-level support to associate deans and related faculty committees. The Assistant Faculty Manager will report to the Faculty Manager or nominee(s) and will liaise closely with the faculty administrative team, Dean, associate deans, heads of academic group, academic colleagues, professional staff in central university units, and relevant university committees on a regular basis. As the implementation of the School Strategy and DCU Operating Model evolves and progresses, the range of duties and responsibilities attached to the post will be subject to change. Please see the attached job description which includes details on the responsibilities associated with the role and the qualifications and experience required. Salary Scale: Professional 6 - €64,984 - €92,573. Appointment will be commensurate with qualifications and experience and in line with current Government pay policy. Closing Date: Friday, 6th November 2026. Additional Information Please note this role does not meet criteria for a Critical Skills permit as stipulated by the Department of Enterprise, Trade & Employment. For more information on DCU Business School, please visit: https://business.dcu.ie/ Informal Enquiries in relation to this role should be directed to: Mr Jonathan Begg, Faculty Manager, DCU Business School, Dublin City University. Phone: + 353 (0)1 7007499 Email: Jonathan.begg@dcu.ie Please do not send applications to this email address, instead apply as described below. Application Procedure: Please submit your application through the online system. In order to be considered for the role to which you are applying for, you must upload: 1) Curriculum Vitae 2) Completed online application form Please note, if all items are not uploaded, the application will be deemed incomplete, and will not be processed. Dublin City University is an equal opportunities employer. In line with the Employment Equality Acts 1998 – 2015, the University is committed to equality of treatment for all those who engage with its recruitment, selection and appointment processes. We will provide support and accommodations to ensure all candidates, including those with disabilities, can fully participate in the process. The University’s Athena Swan Silver Award signifies the University’s commitment to promoting gender equality and addressing any gender pay gaps. Information on a range of university policies aimed at creating a supportive and flexible work environment are available in the DCU Policy Starter Packs talent@dcu.ie +00353 1 700 5436"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/dcurecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=003514": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Children’s Nursing",
      "description": "Closing Date: 08/11/2026 Assistant Professor in Children’s Nursing Applications are invited from suitably qualified candidates for the following position #003514 Assistant Professor in Children’s Nursing, Permanent Contract Role Profile The School is seeking to recruit an Assistant Professor in Children’s Nursing. The successful candidate will be expected to conduct teaching across undergraduate and postgraduate programmes and modules related to children’s nursing through research-led teaching, student mentoring and support, and supervision of student projects and research. The successful candidate will also be expected to support programme administration, clinical placements, research supervision and service and external engagement activities of the School and University. The post is based on the Glasnevin Campus and the successful candidates will report to the Head of School. Please see the attached job description which includes details on the responsibilities associated with the role and the qualifications and experience required. Salary Scale: Assistant Professor (Above Bar) - €65,229 - €104,536 Appointment will be commensurate with qualifications and experience and in line with current Government pay policy. Closing date: Sunday, 8th November 2026 For more information on DCU and our benefits, please visit Why work at DCU? Informal Enquiries in relation to this role should be directed to: Dr Gillian Paul, Head of School of Nursing, Psychotherapy and Community Health, Faculty of Science and Health, Dublin City University. Phone + 353 (0)1 7006156, Email:gillian.paul@dcu.ie Please do not send applications to this email address, instead apply as described below Application Procedure: Please submit your application through the online system. In order to be considered for the role to which you are applying for, you must upload: 1) Curriculum Vitae 2) Cover Letter 3) Completed application form (blank forms can be downloaded from the bottom of the Vacancy). Please note, if all items are not uploaded, the application will be deemed incomplete, and will not be processed. Dublin City University is an equal opportunities employer. In line with the Employment Equality Acts 1998 – 2015, the University is committed to equality of treatment for all those who engage with its recruitment, selection and appointment processes. We will provide support and accommodations to ensure all candidates, including those with disabilities, can fully participate in the process. The University’s Athena Swan Silver Award signifies the University’s commitment to promoting gender equality and addressing any gender pay gaps. Information on a range of university policies aimed at creating a supportive and flexible work environment are available in the DCU Policy Starter Packs #LI-Hybrid Contact Email : talent@dcu.ie Contact No : +00353 1 700 5149"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/dcurecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=003516": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in General Nursing",
      "description": "Closing Date: 08/11/2026 Assistant Professor in General Nursing Applications are invited from suitably qualified candidates for the following position #003516 Assistant Professor in General Nursing, Permanent Contract Role Profile The School is seeking to recruit an Assistant Professor in General Nursing. The successful candidate will be expected to conduct teaching across undergraduate and postgraduate programmes and modules related to general nursing through research-led teaching, student mentoring and support, and supervision of student projects and research. The successful candidate will also be expected to support programme administration, clinical placements, research supervision and service and external engagement activities of the School and University. The post is based on the Glasnevin Campus and the successful candidates will report to the Head of School. Please see the attached job description which includes details on the responsibilities associated with the role and the qualifications and experience required. Salary Scale: Assistant Professor (Above Bar) - €65,229 - €104,536 Appointment will be commensurate with qualifications and experience and in line with current Government pay policy. Closing date: Sunday, 8th November 2026 For more information on DCU and our benefits, please visit Why work at DCU? Informal Enquiries in relation to this role should be directed to: Dr Gillian Paul, Head of School of Nursing, Psychotherapy and Community Health, Faculty of Science and Health, Dublin City University. Phone + 353 (0)1 7006156, Email:gillian.paul@dcu.ie Please do not send applications to this email address, instead apply as described below Application Procedure: Please submit your application through the online system. In order to be considered for the role to which you are applying for, you must upload: 1) Curriculum Vitae 2) Cover Letter 3) Completed application form (blank forms can be downloaded from the bottom of the Vacancy). 