    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/dcurecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=003519": {
      "title": "Associate Professor in General Nursing",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/11/2026 Associate Professor in General Nursing Applications are now invited for the position of #003519 Associate Professor in General Nursing, Permanent Contract Role Profile The School of Nursing, Psychotherapy and Community Health is seeking to recruit exceptionally qualified candidates at the level of Associate Professor in General Nursing. The successful candidate will be recognised as a leader in their discipline, hold a reputation for academic excellence and have an extensive portfolio of internationally competitive research including high impact publications and funding successes. The appointee will be expected to provide leadership to the discipline of general nursing through active engagement in teaching and learning, research and scholarship, and administration in the School, Faculty and University. The appointee will be expected to make outstanding contributions to undergraduate and postgraduate nursing programmes through research-led teaching, student mentoring and support, and supervision of student projects and research. The post holder will be expected to influence research and scholarship in nursing and contribute to advancing the school's research agenda, including building strong partnerships and collaborations with external health service providers. The post is based on the Glasnevin Campus and the candidate will report to the Head of School. Please see the attached job description which outlines the responsibilities associated with the role and the qualifications and experience required. Salary Scale: Associate Professor - €92,001 - €121,262 Appointment will be commensurate with qualifications and experience and in line with current Government pay policy. Closing date : Sunday, 22nd November 2026 For more information on DCU and our benefits, please visit Why work at DCU? Informal Enquiries in relation to this role should be directed to: Dr Gillian Paul, Head of School of Nursing, Psychotherapy and Community Health, Faculty of Science and Health, Dublin City University. Phone + 353 (0)1 7006156, Email: gillian.paul@dcu.ie Please do not send applications to this email address, instead apply as described below. Application Procedure: Please submit your application through the online system at www.dcu.ie/people/jobs, in order to be considered for the role to which you are applying for, you must upload: 1) Curriculum Vitae 2) Cover Letter 3) Completed application form (blank forms can be downloaded from the bottom of the Vacancy). Please note, if all items are not uploaded, the application will be deemed incomplete, and will not be processed. Dublin City University is an equal opportunities employer. In line with the Employment Equality Acts 1998 – 2015, the University is committed to equality of treatment for all those who engage with its recruitment, selection and appointment processes. The University’s Athena Swan Silver Award signifies the University’s commitment to promoting gender equality and addressing any gender pay gaps. Information on a range of university policies aimed at creating a supportive and flexible work environment are available in the DCU Policy Starter Packs #LI-Hybrid Contact Email : talent@dcu.ie Contact No : +00353 1 700 5149"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/dcurecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=003517": {
      "title": "Employee Relations Partner",
      "description": "Closing Date: 06/11/2026 Employee Relations Partner Applications are invited from suitably qualified candidates for the following position #003517 Employee Relations Partner, 3 Year Fixed Term Contract Role Profile The ER Business Partner will report directly to the Senior ER Manager and will assist in ensuring the delivery of both the employee relations and DCU People strategy within the University, its research centres and commercial companies. Please see the attached job description which includes details on the responsibilities associated with the role and the qualifications and experience required. Salary Scale: Professional 5A - €60,580 - €81,922 Appointment will be commensurate with qualifications and experience and in line with current Government pay policy. Closing Date: Friday, 6th November 2026 For more information on DCU and benefits, please visit Why work at DCU? Informal Enquiries in relation to this role should be directed to: Ms Deirdre Reynolds, Senior ER Manager, DCU People, Dublin City University. Email: deirdre.d.reynolds@dcu.ie Please do not send applications to this email address, instead apply as described below. Application Procedure: Please submit your application through the online system. In order to be considered for the role to which you are applying for, you must upload: Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter Completed online application form Please note, if all items are not uploaded, the application will be deemed incomplete, and will not be processed. Dublin City University is an equal opportunities employer. In line with the Employment Equality Acts 1998 – 2015, the University is committed to equality of treatment for all those who engage with its recruitment, selection and appointment processes. We will provide support and accommodations to ensure all candidates, including those with disabilities, can fully participate in the process. The University’s Athena Swan Silver Award signifies the University’s commitment to promoting gender equality and addressing any gender pay gaps. Information on a range of university policies aimed at creating a supportive and flexible work environment are available in the DCU Policy Starter Packs #LI-Hybrid talent@dcu.ie +00353 1 700 5149"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/dcurecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=003513": {
      "title": "Institutional Insights Analyst",
      "description": "Closing Date: 03/11/2026 Institutional Insights Analyst Applications are invited from suitably qualified candidates for the following position # 003513 Institutional Insights Analyst, Permanent Contract Role Profile Reporting to the Director of Quality and Institutional Research the Institutional Insights Analyst will be a key member of a small but vibrant team within the University. The postholder will work closely with the Institutional Research and Analysis Officer to support the delivery of DCU’s institutional research, analytics, and reporting ecosystem. The post holder will support the technical, analytical, and operational aspect of the function—developing high‑quality datasets, dashboards, and leading on statutory returns that underpin evidence‑based decision‑making across the University. The post provides an opportunity for the successful candidate to work across the University, with a broad range of stakeholders in defining and developing reports and dashboards, which will support the enhancement of the student experience, and effectiveness at the University. Please note that shortlisted candidates may be asked to complete a preparatory task as part of the interview process, which may include a piece data analysis or development of a dashboard. Full details will be provided to shortlisted candidates. Please see the attached job description which includes details on the responsibilities associated with the role and the qualifications and experience required. Salary Scale: Professional 5A Salary Scale : €60,580 – €81,922 Appointment will be commensurate with qualifications and experience and in line with current Government pay policy. Closing date: Tuesday, 3rd November 2026 For more information on DCU and benefits, please visit please visit Why to Work in DCU Informal Enquiries in relation to this role should be directed to: Dr Rachel Keegan, Director of Quality and Institutional Research, Quality and Institutional Insights Office, Dublin City University. Email: rachel.keegan@dcu.ie Please do not send applications to this email address, instead apply as described below. Application Procedure: Please submit your application through the online system. In order to be considered for the role to which you are applying for, you must upload: 1) Curriculum Vitae 2) Cover Letter 3) Completed online application form Please note, if all items are not uploaded, the application will be deemed incomplete, and will not be processed. Dublin City University is an equal opportunities employer. In line with the Employment Equality Acts 1998 – 2015, the University is committed to equality of treatment for all those who engage with its recruitment, selection and appointment processes. We will provide support and accommodations to ensure all candidates, including those with disabilities, can fully participate in the process. The University’s Athena Swan Silver Award signifies the University’s commitment to promoting gender equality and addressing any gender pay gaps. Information on a range of university policies aimed at creating a supportive and flexible work environment are available in the DCU Policy Starter Packs #LI-Hybrid talent@dcu.ie +00353 1 700 5149"
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/dcurecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=003520": {
      "title": "Taighdeoir Iardhochtúireachta - Gaois, Fiontar & Scoil na Gaeilge",
      "description": "Closing Date: 06/11/2026 Taighdeoir Iardhochtúireachta - Gaois, Fiontar & Scoil na Gaeilge Applications are invited from suitably qualified candidates for the following position #003520 Postdoctoral Researcher - Gaois, Fiontar & Scoil na Gaeilge, Fixed Term Contract (up to 8 months). Role Profile The successful candidate will be a member of an interdisciplinary team that deals with aspects of terminology, corpus linguistics, toponymy and digital humanities, among other areas. The postdoctoral researcher will work closely with Gaois staff and its research partners to achieve the goals of the projects they are managing. The postdoctoral researcher will be accountable to the Principal Investigators of the various projects and to the Head of School of Fiontar & Scoil na Gaeilge. The person appointed to this particular role will be primarily involved in a research project, funded by Coimisiún na Meán, which involves examining two current affairs programmes from RTE Archives, Féach and Cúrsaí. Please see the attached job description which includes details on the responsibilities associated with the role and the qualifications and experience required. Salary Scale: Post Doctoral Researcher IUA Level 2 - €47,273 - €60,250 (Pro-rata) Appointment will be commensurate with qualifications and experience and in line with current Government pay policy. Closing Date: Friday, 6th November 2026. Additional Information Please note this role does not meet criteria for a Critical Skills permit as stipulated by the Department of Enterprise, Trade & Employment. For more information on the Gaois, Fiontar & Scoil na Gaeilge, please visit - https://www.dcu.ie/fiontarscoilnagaeilge/fiontar-scoil-na-gaeilge Informal Enquiries in relation to this role should be directed to: An Dr Úna Bhreathnach, Fiontar & Scoil na Gaeilge, Dublin City University. Phone + 353 (0)1 7008083 Email: una.bhreathnach@dcu.ie Please do not send applications to this email address, instead apply as described below. Application Procedure: Please submit your application through the online system. In order to be considered for the role to which you are applying for, you must upload: 1) Curriculum Vitae 2) Cover Letter 3) Completed application form (blank forms can be downloaded from the bottom of the Vacancy). Please note, if all items are not uploaded, the application will be deemed incomplete, and will not be processed. Dublin City University is an equal opportunities employer. In line with the Employment Equality Acts 1998 – 2015, the University is committed to equality of treatment for all those who engage with its recruitment, selection and appointment processes. We will provide support and accommodations to ensure all candidates, including those with disabilities, can fully participate in the process. The University’s Athena Swan Silver Award signifies the University’s commitment to promoting gender equality and addressing any gender pay gaps. Information on a range of university policies aimed at creating a supportive and flexible work environment are available in the DCU Policy Starter Packs #LI-Hybrid Contact Email : talent@dcu.ie Contact No : +00353 1 700 5436"
    }
  },
